# docker/.env.example — the operator's configuration surface for the cv-infra
# control plane (M5 §7: "배포 산출물이 곧 계약 표면").
#
# USE:
#   cp docker/.env.example docker/.env      # docker/.env is git-ignored, never committed
#   $EDITOR docker/.env                     # fill in the REQUIRED block below
#   scripts/detect_gpu.sh >> docker/.env    # appends the GPU-adaptive knobs (see below)
#   scripts/consent/accept_eula.sh          # appends the consent keys (do not hand-write them)
#   docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build
#
# Compose loads docker/.env automatically (it is the project directory of
# docker/compose.yaml). Every knob here is read by that compose file; the
# CV_*-prefixed ones are then read by cv_infra.orchestrator.serve inside the
# container (its module docstring is the authoritative env contract).
#
# THREE RULES that will bite you if ignored:
#   1. Every path is a HOST ABSOLUTE path. It is bind-mounted at the SAME path
#      inside the container on purpose — the control plane hands these paths to
#      the host docker daemon when it spawns runners (R-mount, M5 §3.2).
#   2. Create the directories BEFORE the first `up` (`mkdir -p`). Docker creates
#      missing bind sources as root:root, and the runner runs as uid 1234 — the
#      classic silent-permission trap (G-15).
#   3. An empty assignment (`KNOB=`) is NOT the same as a missing line. Optional
#      knobs are loud on set-but-empty by contract (G-26 변종) — comment the line
#      out instead of blanking it.
#
# NFR values (VRAM per instance, concurrency k, throughput) are MEASURED on your
# host, never invented (CLAUDE §2-4). Measurement recipes are noted per knob.

# ============================================================================
# REQUIRED — the control plane refuses to start without these
# ============================================================================

# Runner image pin. NO default exists on purpose (image-as-artifact, FU-10): the
# runner image is built once and recorded, then referenced verbatim. Prefer a
# digest-pinned ref for a release deployment.
#   built by: docker build -f docker/runner/Dockerfile -t cv-infra-runner:<tag> .
CV_RUNNER_IMAGE=cv-infra-runner:<tag-or-@sha256-digest>

# Operator concurrency cap — the AUTHORITATIVE ceiling (LOCKED §7.4). The
# scheduler computes k = min(this, VRAM-derived, ...); it never raises it.
# Start conservative (e.g. 2) and raise it only after measuring per-instance VRAM.
CV_MAX_CONCURRENT=2

# Control-plane state: the SQLite store (job/request/baseline rows) lives at
# ${CV_STATE_DIR}/cv-infra.sqlite3. Keep this path STABLE across restarts and
# re-deploys — regression baselines live in that file (C-1); a changed path
# silently loses your comparison baseline.
CV_STATE_DIR=/home/<operator>/cv-infra-state

# Job artifact root: one subdirectory per job (job_spec.json + result/).
# Sized for MCAP + mp4 recordings — this is the directory that grows.
CV_OUT_DIR=/home/<operator>/cv-infra-out

# Warm Isaac/Omniverse cache base (shader/asset/compute tiers). READ-ONLY source:
# each job gets a `cp -a` copy under the scratch root below (D-B, DoD-P4-15).
# Provisioned/warmed by the workstation scripts; must be owned by uid 1234 so the
# copies stay writable for the runner.
CV_ISAAC_CACHE_ROOT=/home/<operator>/docker/isaac-sim

# Per-job writable cache scratch root. Each job gets its own subtree here, which
# is discarded at job end (stateless, NFR-EXEC-002). Needs room for
# k × (warm cache size) — measure with `du -sh ${CV_ISAAC_CACHE_ROOT}`.
CV_ISAAC_CACHE_SCRATCH_ROOT=/home/<operator>/cv-infra-cache-scratch

# ============================================================================
# CONSENT — written by scripts/consent/accept_eula.sh, NOT by hand
# ============================================================================
# The NVIDIA Isaac Sim EULA / privacy consent keys land in docker/.env only after
# an operator explicitly consented and the consent record (identity + timestamp)
# was written. They are intentionally ABSENT from this example file: nothing in
# this repository may carry an acceptance value (NEG-2 / LOCKED §8), and no CI
# job may synthesize one to skip the step.
#
#   scripts/consent/accept_eula.sh        # present -> consent -> record -> .env
#   scripts/consent/check_consent.sh      # gate: exit 0 = recorded, exit 3 = not
#
# Consequence worth knowing: because compose requires these keys, every compose
# subcommand (`up`, `down`, `ps`) needs them present in docker/.env. Stop the
# stack BEFORE removing consent from a host.
#
# AFTER consent has been given, docker/.env holds something ONLY AN OPERATOR CAN
# PRODUCE. From that point on, never RECREATE the file to change a knob — no
# `cp .env.example .env`, no `> docker/.env`, no editor "save as new file". That
# silently deletes the consent and the only repair is calling the operator back.
# Edit it with an idempotent single-line upsert instead (the same idiom
# accept_eula.sh uses: drop that ONE key's line, append the new one,
# `install -m 600`), then re-run scripts/consent/check_consent.sh to prove the
# consent survived. Appending (`scripts/detect_gpu.sh >> docker/.env`) is safe.
# Full recipe: docs/deploy/README.md §3.

# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — leave commented out unless you measured/need them
# ============================================================================

# Per-instance VRAM in MiB. Setting it turns ON the NVML second guard, so k also
# respects live free VRAM. MEASURE it, never guess:
#   per-PID `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=pid,used_memory` during a single job,
#   cross-checked against the full (G+C) process table, minus idle baseline
#   (DoD-P2-09 / DoD-P4-10 recipe).
# You do not have to do that by hand on a GPU this project has already measured:
#   scripts/detect_gpu.sh >> docker/.env
# identifies the live card, picks the matching profiles/*.yaml and writes this line
# WITH the evidence path the number came from (REQ-DEPLOY-003; docs/deploy/gpu-profiles.md).
# For a supported-but-unmeasured card it deliberately writes the line COMMENTED OUT —
# the guard stays off rather than running on a guessed budget.
#CV_VRAM_PER_INSTANCE_MB=

# Outer wall-clock cap (seconds) over the WHOLE job, image pulls included — the
# only bound that spans a crawling registry pull. Unset = the measured default
# compiled into the code. If you set it, it MUST stay strictly greater than the
# inner container watchdog or the coherence gate refuses at first submit.
#CV_OUTER_WALLCLOCK_S=

# Control-plane image. Default = locally built `cv-infra-orchestrator:local`.
# Pin to a registry ref (@sha256) once you publish one.
#CV_ORCHESTRATOR_IMAGE=

# Compose project name (container/network name prefix). Change it only if you run
# two independent cv-infra deployments on one host.
#CV_COMPOSE_PROJECT=cv-infra

# Where the submit API is published on the HOST. Default 127.0.0.1:8000.
# The API has no authentication (single-host MVP) — publishing it on 0.0.0.0
# exposes job submission to your network. Do that only behind a trusted boundary.
#CV_PUBLISH_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#CV_PUBLISH_PORT=8000

# Container log rotation (json-file driver). Defaults: 50m × 5 files.
#CV_LOG_MAX_SIZE=50m
#CV_LOG_MAX_FILE=5
